  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 1,
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CoCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>,</p><p>(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CoCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#183;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>,</p><p>(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CoCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#183;</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> and</p><p>(d)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CoCl</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#183;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p><p>Number of complex(es) which will exist in cis-trans form is/are</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "1",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Cis-trans isomerism is a type of geometrical isomerism.This type of isomerism occurs when there is a change in spatial arrangements of ligands. Cis and trans are the two terms used for representing the position of identical ligands when they are placed adjacent and opposite</p><p>(i)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CoCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Co</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mi>Cl</mi></math>, can&nbsp;exhibit cis-trans&nbsp;isomerism.</p><p>(ii)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CoCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Co</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>5</mn></msub><mi>Cl</mi></mrow></mfenced><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>,can&#39;t exhibit cis-trans&nbsp; isomerism.</p><p>(iii) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CoCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Co</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>6</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>,can&#39;t exhibit cis-trans&nbsp; isomerism.</p><p>(IV)</p><p>&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CoCl</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Co</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>5</mn></msub><mi>Cl</mi></mrow></mfenced><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>or</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mi>Co</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>5</mn></msub><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mrow></mrow></msub><mi>Cl</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace></math></p><p>Both can&#39;t exhibit cis-trans&nbsp; isomerism.</p><p>Hence. only one complex exhibits cis-trans isomerism</p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
